# The sources plinux actually builds. One line per entry in packages/order,
# in the same order.
#
# This is not the LFS list. wget-list-sysv is the book's own file and is 91
# entries, of which about sixty are the temporary toolchain -- gcc, binutils,
# gmp, mpfr, mpc, m4, make, perl, python, tcl, expect, autoconf, libtool,
# texinfo -- that LFS builds in chapters 5 and 6 so that chapter 8 can be
# built inside a chroot. plinux does none of that: it builds against the host
# toolchain and stages into obj/, so downloading those tarballs fetches
# several hundred megabytes that nothing ever unpacks.
#
# wget-list-sysv is kept in the tree, unused by default, because it is the
# starting point if the self-hosting toolchain ever gets built. Fetch it with
# ./download.sh --list wget-list-sysv.
#
# Two things the build needs are not here, because they do not come from a
# tarball: the kernel and pboot are git checkouts under src/, made by
# ./configure.
#
# musl used to be a third. It sat in src/musl, unpacked by hand, with no
# recorded origin -- which meant a fresh clone could not build the image and
# nothing said so until packages/musl.sh failed on a missing directory. It is
# a tarball like everything else now.

# the C libraries. glibc first: udev is part of systemd and systemd does not
# build against musl, so both are installed and every binary names the one
# it wants.
https://ftpmirror.gnu.org/glibc/glibc-2.42.tar.xz
https://www.linuxfromscratch.org/patches/lfs/12.4/glibc-2.42-fhs-1.patch

# musl 1.2.5, matching the /musl installation this workstation compiles the
# p* components and bash against.
#
# Not from musl.libc.org, which is where it comes from and which does not
# resolve from this machine -- wget stops at "unable to resolve host address"
# while every other entry in this file downloads. The copy here is MacPorts'
# distfiles mirror, which serves upstream's tarball under upstream's name.
#
# It is the same file: MacPorts' copy and Debian's musl_1.2.5.orig.tar.gz are
# byte for byte identical, sha256 a9a118bbe84d8764da0ea0d28b3ab3fae8477fc7e
# 4085d90102b8596fc7c75e4, which is upstream's published checksum. Two
# distributors with no reason to agree is the best confirmation available
# while the origin is unreachable. If MacPorts ever goes, Debian's pool has
# the same bytes under a different name:
#
#   https://ftp.debian.org/debian/pool/main/m/musl/musl_1.2.5.orig.tar.gz musl-1.2.5.tar.gz
https://distfiles.macports.org/musl/musl-1.2.5.tar.gz

# the shell. Static against musl, so the login path -- pinit execs plogin,
# plogin execs this -- does not depend on the loader.
https://ftpmirror.gnu.org/bash/bash-5.3.tar.gz

# The build tools, and the toolchain.
#
# None of these were here while packages were compiled on the host and staged
# into obj/, because the host supplied them and the image only had to run
# what came out. Building inside the chroot ended that: there is no host in
# there, and coreutils stopped on a missing automake within four packages.
#
# pkgconf is the one that would have stopped everything. packages/common.sh
# aims PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR at the image, and nothing from libffi onward -- the
# whole Wayland stack -- configures without it.
#
# bc and flex are wanted by other packages' build systems; libtool, autoconf
# and automake by anything that regenerates its build system after a patch,
# which is what coreutils does.
https://distfiles.ariadne.space/pkgconf/pkgconf-2.5.1.tar.xz
https://github.com/gavinhoward/bc/releases/download/7.0.3/bc-7.0.3.tar.xz
https://github.com/westes/flex/releases/download/v2.6.4/flex-2.6.4.tar.gz
https://ftpmirror.gnu.org/libtool/libtool-2.5.4.tar.xz
https://ftpmirror.gnu.org/gperf/gperf-3.3.tar.gz
https://ftpmirror.gnu.org/autoconf/autoconf-2.72.tar.xz
https://ftpmirror.gnu.org/automake/automake-1.18.1.tar.xz

